        :note "admit-build closes the omission hole: verify-locked-definitions alone returns ok? true when a build presents nothing, which is the mutable-name fallback reached by omission. A dependency pinning definition CIDs must now actually resolve all of them."}
  :ci5 {:status :implemented
        :evidence ["src/kotoba/lang/capability_values.cljc"
                   "src/kotoba/lang/capability_host.cljc"
                   "lang/capability-conformance/positive/component_binding_dispatch.edn"
                   "lang/capability-conformance/negative/component_binding_not_a_component_capability.edn"
                   "lang/capability-conformance/negative/component_binding_host_binding_guard_mismatch.edn"
                   "kotoba-lang/kotoba-component src/kotoba/component/wit.cljc (emit :host-binding)"]
        :note "Both halves. Emit: kotoba.component.wit/emit returns :host-binding {:contract :kotoba.component.host-binding/v1 :required-guard :guard-component-ability-call :imports {<import> <guard>}} covering every effectful import, on both the typed-capability-v3 and default branches, without perturbing the WIT text or its sha256. Host: bind-component-imports reads that declaration and refuses an undeclared, mismatched, or incomplete one, alongside a non-component target, a handler for an undeclared import, and a declared import left unbound. Each bound entry supplies :call and :handler itself, so a caller cannot substitute a weaker guard. The weaker guards stay public for non-component hosts -- guard-ability-call falls through to guard-call for a request that is not a capability value and skips the effect gate, which guard-component-ability-call denies as :component-ability-invalid; a negative fixture pins that difference."
        :residual-risk ["A host that ignores bind-component-imports and calls a guard directly is still unconstrained; this makes the correct route checkable, not mandatory at the language level."
                        "The declaration binds the host entry point, not the provider implementation behind it; provider-side resource revalidation remains its own obligation."]}
  :ci6 {:status :implemented
        :evidence ["scripts/verify_code_identity_cljs.cljs"
                   "scripts/verify_capability_conformance_cljs.cljs"]
        :note "All three layers are covered. Identity: all 10 frozen vectors produce identical canonical bytes and CIDs under ClojureScript (nbb) and Clojure -- this runner is what found that integer literals past 2^53-1 are rounded by the cljs reader before any encoder runs, which the i64 exact form now refuses rather than hides. Capability: all 24 conformance cases (component-binding, host-dispatch, intersection, effects, receipts, and cacao grant/revocation) reach the same verdict under both. Because the manifest pins the expected denial reason per negative case, agreement means the same reason fired, not merely that both denied -- verified by a negative control that corrupts one expected reason and observes exactly one disagreement."
        :wit-closed "The typed-capability-v3 WIT profile is portable as of kotoba-lang/abi 4172242: kotoba.abi.contract/typed-capability-wit-v3 no longer reads a classpath resource, so ClojureScript produces the world instead of rejecting. Measured byte-identical across implementations -- 5042 chars, sha256 387f1e6db6408aff5485ad7bf484b691a86562c8858be29b846a52fa5b9efa02, on both nbb and the JVM. A filesystem read would not have worked, since abi is consumed as a git dependency and a consumer runs from its own root; the bytes are embedded in a generated kotoba.abi.wit-data and kotoba.abi.wit-data-test asserts the embedded copy still equals its file."
                        :wit-evidence "kotoba-lang/kotoba-component: src/kotoba/component/wit.cljc, lang/wit-vectors.edn, scripts/verify_wit_cljs.cljs"
        :wit-note "The WIT layer is now comparable and compared. kotoba.component.wit was ported from .clj to .cljc -- only two of its 344 lines were JVM-bound (contract loading and SHA-256), while the 340 that matter, WIT identifier canonicalization and the Kotoba->WIT type mapping, were already portable. Five frozen vectors covering a pure export, one capability, two capabilities sharing an interface, capabilities spanning interfaces in non-sorted call order, and the scalar/string type mapping all emit byte-identical text and identical digests under Clojure and ClojureScript. Negative control: corrupting one import name in the table yields 3 disagreements across 5 vectors."
        :residual-risk ["Receipts are compared field-wise by the checkers, not hashed. If receipt bytes ever become a cross-implementation contract, they need their own frozen-vector table like the identity one."
                        "Quota and deadline enforcement is asserted by the capability fixtures at the policy layer; it is not separately measured against a running provider under both implementations."]}
  :ci7 {:status :implemented
        :evidence ["kotoba-lang/amu: src/kotoba/compiler/project.cljc (capability-operation?)"
                   "kotoba-lang/amu: test/kotoba/compiler/ci7_elaboration_parity_test.clj"]
        :note "Asking the question found that friendly capability operations did not elaborate in project builds -- they did not compile at all. project/link-source treated every namespaced call as a module import and rejected (clock/now seed) with 'qualified call is not an admitted exported import', while the identical single-file source compiled. capability-operation? now consults the capability registry, with module imports still resolved first so an explicit :require alias always wins over a capability of the same name. With that fixed the parity holds: same wire id, same typed ability call, same inferred effect closure, same admission verdict, and same missing grant under an empty policy on both paths. The admission rule also holds -- no source in the fixtures names a capability id or a callback."
        :provenance-note "Provenance was initially dropped by linking: modules are analyzed before linking, so the emitted body was already (typed-cap-call <id> ...) and :named-operations came out empty for a project build where a single file reported the operation. project/resugar-capability-calls now restores the source spelling from the :source-operation metadata the elaborated form carries, so both paths record identical provenance. It runs before rewrite-calls (which rebuilds forms with list* and would drop that metadata) and rewrites only forms carrying :source-operation, so module-to-module calls keep their stubs and synthetic names and per-module isolation is unchanged."
        :source-map-note "link-source returns :source-map and project/source-position translates a linked line to {:module :source-name :source-span}. source-text emits one form per line and the ns form is line 1, so an emitted function's linked line is its index + 2; the module-relative span is captured in analyze-module before rewrite-calls, which rebuilds forms with list* and drops metadata. Synthetic forms -- the ns form, project dispatchers, export wrappers -- have no authoring module and are absent from the map rather than attributed to one."
        :residual-risk ["Module and function attribution is total for emitted module functions, but the fine-grained span is only present where the frontend attached one -- it does so for capability calls, not for every body. A consumer therefore gets file-level attribution always and line-level only where a span exists; module-attribution-is-total-even-where-a-span-is-not pins that, so a nil span reads as a known limit rather than a broken map."
                        "Nothing consumes the source map yet. Diagnostics raised against a project build still carry linked-source spans; translating them at the point they are rendered is a separate change in whatever surfaces the error."]}
